  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nFrom Sunday through the end of the period, both deterministic \nguidance and global ensembles keep upper-level ridging across the \nwestern CONUS, with northwesterly flow persisting over Middle \nMississippi Valley. However, mid-level ridging will build into the \nMiddle Mississippi, with southwesterly flow advecting anomalously \nwarm air into the region. While the CWA will have already sampled \nsome of this heat on Saturday, the true brunt of it will be realized \nstarting Sunday. Then, guidance consensus is that 850 mb \ntemperatures will top out around in the low 20 degrees C, with \nvalues around 25 degrees C on Monday. This climatologically favors \nsurface temperatures in the mid 90s on Sunday and then upper 90s to \naround 100 degrees on Monday. NBM dew points in the mid to upper 70s \nseems bold with this degree of heat; however, these values are \nsupported by a majority of ensemble members. This will produce \nwidespread heat index values of 105-110 degrees, if not higher. \n\nVariability in the placement of the mid-level ridge increases on \nTuesday, though mid-level temperatures generally hold steady. There \nis a portion of guidance that also sends at least a weak front \nthrough the area that has the potential to take the edge off the \nheat. However, with the low to mid-levels favoring another day of \noppressive heat, an Excessive Heat Watch has been issued for Sunday \nthrough Tuesday. \n\nEnsemble consensus is that 850 mb temperatures gradually cool as the \nwestern ridge stays in place Wednesday through Friday, though the \nexact phasing differs among ensemble clusters. These phasing \ndifferences paired with the threat of additional disturbances within \nthe northwesterly flow aloft lead to uncertainty in how warm \ntemperatures will remain. A warmer solution favors high Wednesday-\nFriday in the mid 90s, while a cooler solution supports highs \nthrough that stretch in the upper 80s to around 90 degrees. The \ncurrent forecast does have temperatures gradually cooling, and \nsplits the difference between the two solutions.",
